00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:11.340
Overhyped games that ended up being terrible.

00:00:11.769 --> 00:00:17.080
Nothing's worse than spending $60 of your
hard-earned cash on a new game, only to be

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:22.090
disappointed with the product. We've all been
burnt by bad games at one point or another.

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:27.470
Publishers and developers overhype their games,
making bold promises, and placing false hope

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:32.330
into the minds and hearts of consumers. Here
are a few of the worst offenders.

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:34.820
Final Fantasy 14 Online

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:40.260
Despite amazing graphics and music, the first
version of Final Fantasy 14 Online turned

00:00:40.269 --> 00:00:45.909
out to be a massive failure. Much like a cake
that ends up raw in the middle, the game looked

00:00:45.909 --> 00:00:51.659
good on the outside, but was mostly unfinished.
Reviews and complaints got so bad that Square

00:00:51.659 --> 00:00:56.129
Enix pulled the plug on subscriptions and
started over from scratch, crafting a whole

00:00:56.129 --> 00:01:01.979
new game. The resulting reboot, Final Fantasy
14: A Realm Reborn, took place five years

00:01:01.979 --> 00:01:07.540
after the original. Reborn was well-received
by both gamers and critics alike. Now let

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:12.830
us never speak of the failure that was Final
Fantasy 14 Online again.

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:15.160
Sonic the Hedgehog (2006)

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:20.380
The 15th anniversary of Sonic the Hedgehog
came in 2006, and to mark this event, Sega

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:25.980
released a game called…well, it was just
called Sonic the Hedgehog. In it, the rapid

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:31.390
rodent was tasked with saving a princess named
Elise from Doctor Eggman. Unfortunately, the

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:35.800
developers took a page out of Sonic's book
and rushed it. The end product was a clunky

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:41.040
game with poor controls, long load times,
and a host of glitches. Sonic's all about

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:45.120
speed, but there are some times when you'll
want to take it slow.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.450
Star Wars: The Force Unleashed 2

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:52.180
The adventures of the anti-hero known as Starkiller
continued in Star Wars: The Force Unleashed

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:57.530
2. Now, you'd think that getting to control
Darth Vader's secret apprentice, a dude who

00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:03.140
wields the Force with unimaginable strength,
would be a joy. But you would be wrong. Even

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:07.940
though it captured the same size and scope
of the original, The Force Unleashed 2 failed

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:12.909
to deliver a good story and was bogged down
by repetitive combat. It's cool to hack at

00:02:12.909 --> 00:02:17.180
enemies and Mind Trick them to their own doom.
But when that's pretty much all you're doing

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:22.380
amid a weak story, it kills the fun. This
was not the sequel you were looking for.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:23.340
Knack

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:28.260
One of the PlayStation 4's launch titles,
Knack had the duty of setting up the console's

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:32.760
future and success. Let's take a moment to
thank Sony that it wasn't the only launch

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:37.590
title for the PS4, because that would have
been a disaster. Knack looked like it could've

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:43.329
been fun. But nope. The gameplay was terrible:
players would die from the smallest encounters

00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:48.139
and have to backtrack a ton. Not only was
the gameplay frustrating, but the story was

00:02:48.139 --> 00:02:52.999
so weak that your rage at the crappy gameplay
was canceled out by your utter boredom.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:55.000
Tony Hawk: Ride

00:02:55.819 --> 00:03:01.010
Prior to its release, Tony Hawk: Ride was
touted as the next major advancement in video

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:05.249
game interaction, giving players a physical
board accessory that would transfer your movement

00:03:05.249 --> 00:03:10.129
into the game. But...it didn't work. And we
don't mean it didn't work well. It didn't

00:03:10.129 --> 00:03:15.060
work at all. There was such a delay between
what players did on the board and what happened

00:03:15.069 --> 00:03:17.569
in the game that it was basically unplayable.

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:19.480
Ryse: Son of Rome

00:03:19.939 --> 00:03:25.510
Developed by Crytek, the makers of the incredible
Crysis series, Ryse: Son of Rome was set to

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:31.049
be one of the Xbox One's coolest Kinect titles.
Players took on the role of a Roman centurion

00:03:31.049 --> 00:03:36.189
as he embarks on a blood-soaked quest for
revenge. But during development, Ryse underwent

00:03:36.189 --> 00:03:42.090
a bunch of changes that saw it go from a Kinect-heavy
first-person title to a third-person hack-and-slash

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:46.909
game. When it was finally released, Ryse was
beautiful, but repetitive. Considering it

00:03:46.909 --> 00:03:51.219
was a launch title for the Xbox One, it should've
been much more than just a button-mashing

00:03:51.219 --> 00:03:52.019
bore.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:53.240
Haze

00:03:53.549 --> 00:03:58.719
Prior to its release, Haze was heralded as
a "Halo-killer." But all it ended up killing

00:03:58.719 --> 00:04:04.400
were our hopes for a good game. This first-person
shooter cast players as Shane Carpenter, a

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:09.370
soldier employed by a corporation called Mantel.
Mantel produces a drug called "Nectar" that

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:13.889
allows soldiers to perform better in battle,
but also warps their sense of reality with

00:04:13.889 --> 00:04:18.949
hallucinations. Between its unimpressive level
design, lame story, and its underwhelming

00:04:18.949 --> 00:04:23.430
gameplay, Haze's developers must have been
the ones hallucinating, thinking they could

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:24.850
topple Halo.

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:26.720
Duke Nukem Forever

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:32.520
Stuck stewing in development hell for about
15 years, Duke Nukem Forever was finally released

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:38.650
in 2011. Unfortunately, the long-awaited sequel
to Duke Nukem 3D had gamers wanting to send

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:44.210
it back where it came from. Simply put, it
felt dated. It was so bad that all versions

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:49.620
of the game scored in the 50s on sites like
Metacritic and GameRankings. In the end, this

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:54.889
sequel's dismal reviews nearly tainted the
memory of the original. Not groovy.

00:04:54.889 --> 00:04:57.080
Aliens: Colonial Marines

00:04:57.080 --> 00:05:02.699
Probably the biggest offender on this list,
Aliens: Colonial Marines was so awful that

00:05:02.699 --> 00:05:07.949
fans were unanimously outraged by its badness.
Some say it's one of the worst games to ever

00:05:07.949 --> 00:05:13.139
be released by Sega. The graphics were lousy,
it had shoddy multiplayer, incredibly stupid

00:05:13.139 --> 00:05:19.310
A.I., unbalanced gameplay, and terrible sound.
The developer, Gearbox Software, also claimed

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:23.979
that the story was going to be in continuity
with the films, but its lousy script revealed

00:05:23.979 --> 00:05:29.039
that to be total nonsense. It was so poorly
received, in fact, that angry gamers actually

00:05:29.039 --> 00:05:31.699
filed a lawsuit against Sega and Gearbox.

00:05:31.699 --> 00:05:37.139
If you find yourself unfortunate to come in
contact with a copy of Aliens: Colonial Marines,

00:05:37.139 --> 00:05:39.039
there's only one appropriate reaction:

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:41.639
Game over man, game over!

00:05:41.639 --> 00:05:43.099
Goodnight, everybody.

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:47.460
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to see more videos like the one you

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:51.720
just saw. And leave us a comment to tell us
which overhyped games you think should've

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:52.580
made this list...

